window.SAQ_PAPER_QUESTIONS["2024-1-northern-sydney"]=[{"number":1,"title":"","stemText":"Emergency physicians make clinical decisions multiple times daily. In some circumstances medical errors occur because of cognitive biases and flaws in clinical reasoning. Decision-making theory distinguishes two systems of thought - “System 1” and “System 2”.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Provide a comparison of these two systems, listing their key characteristics, advantages and disadvantages (6 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"Provide four (4) examples (4 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Advantage</th><th>Disadvantage</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>System 1</td><td>System 2</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Familiar situations</td><td>Uncertain, unfamiliar</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Relies on prior experience/learning</td><td>Relies on pursuit of information</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Fast, efficient</td><td>Slow</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Unconscious, automatic</td><td>Deliberate</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Pattern recognition</td><td>Logical, analytical</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>A number of cognitive biases that adversely affect ED decision-making have been</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>described.</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>b. Provide an explanation/definition for:</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>1. Confirmation bias (2 marks)</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>the tendency to look for confirming evidence to support a diagnosis rather</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>than look for disconfirming evidence to refute it, despite the latter often</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>being more persuasive and definitive</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>2. Base-rate neglect (2 marks)</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>the tendency to ignore the true prevalence of a disease, either inflating or</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>reducing its base-rate, and distorting Bayesian reasoning.</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>3. Search satisficing (2 marks)</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>reflects the universal tendency to call off a search once something is found.</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>It is pervasive and considered one of the most important sources of error in</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>radiology. Comorbidities, second foreign bodies, other fractures, and co-</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>ingestants in poisoning may all be missed.</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Conditions that increase the production of errors are common in emergency</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>departments. Some are inherent in the practice of emergency medicine -</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>others are largely due to the way in which the ED is designed and</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>operated.</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">High decision density - High levels of diagnostic uncertainty - High cognitive</div><div class=\"answer-line\">load  - Narrow time windows for assessment - Multiple transitions of care -</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Multitasking - Interruptions/distractions - Low signal to noise ratio - Surge</div><div class=\"answer-line\">phenomena - Fatigue - Circadian dyssynchronicity - Sleep deprivation/debt -</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Novel or infrequently occurring conditions</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Suboptimal ED design - Suboptimal ergonomics - High communication load -</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Overcrowding - Cognitive overloading - Holding admitted patients -</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Production pressures - High noise levels - Inadequate staffing - Poor</div><div class=\"answer-line\">feedback - Limited resources</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":2,"title":"","stemText":"A 57-year-old woman presents with 3 days of watery diarrhoea and vomiting.\nShe has a background of type-2 diabetes managed with metformin. She is confused and unwell in the ED.\n\nHR 110\n\nBP 70/40\nAn arterial blood gas is obtained.\n\npH 6.62\n\npCO2 15.3 mmHg\n\npO2 165 mmHg\n\nK 6.4 mmol/L\n\nNa 135 mmol/L\n\nCl 116 mmol/L\nGlu 2.6 mmol/L\n\nCr 710\n\nLactate 15.2 mmol/L\nBicarb 2.4 mmol/L","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Describe and interpret the blood gas (4 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"What is the role of calculating a delta gap / delta ratio? (you are not required to do the calculation) (2 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"List three other drugs (from three separate classes) and three non-drug causes of Type B lactic acidosis (6 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Primary Metabolic Acidosis, with: Secondary Respiratory Alkalosis and</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Additional Non-Gap Metabolic Acidosis.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Expected pCO2 = 1.5 x Bicarb + 8 +/- 2</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Anion gap = Na - (Cl + Bicarb) = 135 - 118.4 = 16.6</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Delta gap = AG - 12 = 4.6</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Delta ratio = Delta Gap/24-Bicarb = 0.2 = additional hyperchloraemic normal</div><div class=\"answer-line\">anion gap metabolic acidosis</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Can check delta ratio in the presence of a high anion gap metabolic acidosis</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">(HAGMA) to determine if it is a ‘pure’ HAGMA or if there is coexistant normal</div><div class=\"answer-line\">anion gap metabolic acidosis (NAGMA) or metabolic alkalosis.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Metformin is considered a likely precipitant for the high lactate in this patient.</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Type B – No Evidence of Inadequate Tissue Oxygen Delivery</div><div class=\"answer-line\">B1: associated with underlying diseases</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">o LUKE: leukaemia, lymphoma</div><div class=\"answer-line\">o TIPS: thiamine deficiency, infection, pancreatitis, short bowel</div><div class=\"answer-line\">syndrome</div><div class=\"answer-line\">o FAILURES: hepatic, renal, diabetic failures</div><div class=\"answer-line\">B2: associated with drugs &amp; toxins</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">o phenformin</div><div class=\"answer-line\">o cyanide</div><div class=\"answer-line\">o beta-agonists</div><div class=\"answer-line\">o methanol</div><div class=\"answer-line\">o adrenaline</div><div class=\"answer-line\">o salicylates</div><div class=\"answer-line\">o nitroprusside infusion</div><div class=\"answer-line\">o ethanol intoxication in chronic alcoholics</div><div class=\"answer-line\">o anti-retroviral drugs</div><div class=\"answer-line\">o paracetamol</div><div class=\"answer-line\">o salbutamol</div><div class=\"answer-line\">o biguanides</div><div class=\"answer-line\">o fructose</div><div class=\"answer-line\">o sorbitol</div><div class=\"answer-line\">o xylitol</div><div class=\"answer-line\">o isoniazid</div><div class=\"answer-line\">o lactate-based dialysate in RRT</div><div class=\"answer-line\">o congenital forms of lactic acidosis with various enzyme defects — e.g.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">pyruvate carboxylase deficiency, glucose-6-phosphatase and</div><div class=\"answer-line\">fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase deficiencies, oxidative phosphorylation</div><div class=\"answer-line\">enzyme defects)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">B3: associated with inborn errors of metabolism</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":3,"title":"","stemText":"A 77-year-old female is brought in by ambulance after becoming acutely vertiginous getting out of bed and falling, striking her face on a door frame.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Regarding the evaluation of vertigo, list three (3) bedside diagnostic tests and the findings that ‘rule in’ a posterior circulation stroke. (6 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"The patient undergoes a CT Brain (images below) describe the abnormalities and interpret the findings (4 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"How would you clinically confirm your concerns and what would the reported finding be. (3 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">tests and the findings that ‘rule in’ a posterior circulation stroke. (6</div><div class=\"answer-line\">marks)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Model Answer</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Head Impulse (1) – Absence of saccade (1)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Nystagmus (1) – Any vertical nystagmus or horizontal nystagmus that</div><div class=\"answer-line\">changes direction with lateral gaze (bidirectional) (1)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Test of Skew (1) – Vertical skew (correction) (1)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Kattah JC, Talkad AV, Wang DZ, Hsieh YH, Newman-Toker DE. HINTS to diagnose</div><div class=\"answer-line\">stroke in the acute vestibular syndrome: three-step bedside oculomotor</div><div class=\"answer-line\">examination more sensitive than early MRI diffusion-weighted imaging. Stroke.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2009;40:3504–3510.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">The patient undergoes a CT Brain (images below)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Axial</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Coronal</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Blow out fracture of medial orbital wall (Lamina papyracea) (1)</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Radiological herniation of medial rectus (1)</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Plus one relevant negative : No radiological proptosis / no other fractures</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">identified (1)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Interpretation – Entrapment / tethering of medial rectus (1)</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">How would you clinically confirm your concerns and what would the</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Examination of extra-ocular eye muscles (1)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Opthalmoplegia – unable to fully abduct Rt eye (1)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Diplopia – Horizontal on lateral gaze (1)</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":4,"title":"","stemText":"A 25-year-old woman is brought to your ED following a bee sting.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Provide the Australian Society of clinical Immunology and Allergy (ASCIA) definition of anaphylaxis (4 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"Outline how the test is performed and its utility. (4 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"Outline the modifications to standard care you would make in an unconscious, peri-arrest anaphylaxis scenario (4 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Any acute onset illness with typical skin features (urticarial rash or</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">erythema/flushing, and/or angioedema), plus involvement of respiratory</div><div class=\"answer-line\">and/or cardiovascular and/or persistent severe gastrointestinal symptoms;</div><div class=\"answer-line\">or</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Any acute onset of hypotension or bronchospasm or upper airway</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">obstruction where anaphylaxis is considered possible, even if typical skin</div><div class=\"answer-line\">features are not present.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">A mast cell tryptase assay and convalescent sample is sometimes requested in the</div><div class=\"answer-line\">setting of an allergic reaction.</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Anaphylaxis is a clinical diagnosis but if there is doubt or a need for confirmation</div><div class=\"answer-line\">(e.g. diagnostic uncertainty - differential like vasovagal, carcinoid, phaeo etc) mast</div><div class=\"answer-line\">cell tryptase can be useful. Rises in 30-120 minutes post event. A convalescent</div><div class=\"answer-line\">sample a week later (or an assay done on stored serum from a sample sent for</div><div class=\"answer-line\">another reason pre-event) needs to be sent to distinguish from conditions in which</div><div class=\"answer-line\">tryptase is chronically elevated (e.g. systemic mastocytosis, AML, myelodysplasia,</div><div class=\"answer-line\">renal failure)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Fatal anaphylaxis usually occurs within one hour of allergen exposure and</div><div class=\"answer-line\">frequently from extreme airway oedema, bronchospasm and respiratory arrest.</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">From AMAX4</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Early IV adrenaline push-doses (e.g. 1 mcg/kg) +/- infusion</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Early endotracheal intubation - may need high pressures - BVM/LMA not</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">sufficient</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Ventilate with bag (not machine) small volumes, expect high pressures, long</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">I:E</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Aggressive bronchodilators (salbutamol, Mg, steroids) and vasopressors</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Exclude pneumothorax</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Early move to ECMO</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":5,"title":"","stemText":"A 62-year-old woman slips over on her driveway and presents with a painful right ankle.\nHer x-rays are shown below.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"State three (3) relevant findings on the x-ray (3 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"Other than DVT prophylaxis, list three (3) steps in your management (3 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"She is concerned because a friend of hers had a DVT when she broke her leg. List six (6) potential risk factors for DVT in this lady: (6 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">(must have both bolded items for full marks)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">1) comminuted mid shaft fibular fracture</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2) distal tibial fracture involving the posterior malleolus</div><div class=\"answer-line\">3) tibial fracture extends to the joint margin</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Soft tissue swelling medial aspect of ankle</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Ankle mortise undisrupted</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">(must have both bolded items for full marks)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">1) immobilise in below knee backslab</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2) elevate</div><div class=\"answer-line\">3) analgesia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Admit orthopaedics /  refer orthopaedics / keep NBM / non-weight bearing</div><div class=\"answer-line\">She is concerned because a friend of hers had a DVT when she broke her leg.</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Will not accept pregnant or post partum (she is 62!)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Personal hx VTE</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Active cancer or cancer treatment</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Known thrombophilia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Age &gt; 60</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Obesity BMI &gt;30</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Significant comorbidity such as CCF / COPD</div><div class=\"answer-line\">First deg relative with hx VTE</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Use of HRT, tamoxifen (not OCP)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Varicose veins with phlebitis</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Smoker</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Being in a rigid cast or cam boot</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Being non weight bearing</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Having a fracture</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":6,"title":"","stemText":"A 2-year-old ingested a button battery approximately an hour ago.\nDetail the main mechanism of oesophageal injury in button battery ingestion (3 marks) The flow of electric current from the battery through the surrounding tissue occurs near the negative pole and can cause local hydrolysis of H2O, hydroxide accumulation, and liquefactive corrosive tissue injury","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List the battery features and patient factors that inform your risk assessment in button battery ingestion (5 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"On plain X-ray, how can you distinguish between an ingested coin and a button battery? (2 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"If there is a delay to endoscopic removal of an oesophageal button battery, outline other measures that can be taken to slow oesophageal injury (2 marks)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"Outline your discharge instructions (4 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">The flow of electric current from the battery through the surrounding tissue</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">occurs near the negative pole and can cause local hydrolysis of H2O,</div><div class=\"answer-line\">hydroxide accumulation, and liquefactive corrosive tissue injury</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Agent &amp; dose - battery features</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Voltage - more is worse. Need at least 1.2V to cause hydrolysis.Used</div><div class=\"answer-line\">o</div><div class=\"answer-line\">batteries still carry residual charge</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Battery size - batteries &gt;20mm diameter more likely to get stuck and</div><div class=\"answer-line\">o</div><div class=\"answer-line\">cause injury.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Time since ingestion - the critical cut-off for removal is 2 hours</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Clinical features so far</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Early features from obstruction - vomiting/food refusal, drooling,</div><div class=\"answer-line\">o</div><div class=\"answer-line\">airway noises/wheeze, cough, choke, gag</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Early features from oesophageal burn injury - chest/abdo pain (often</div><div class=\"answer-line\">o</div><div class=\"answer-line\">just grunting), fever</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Patient factors</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Age is an epidemiological risk  - most ingestions &lt; 4 years (peak 1-2</div><div class=\"answer-line\">o</div><div class=\"answer-line\">years)</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">More than half of fatalities are due to mis-identification of battery vs coin. A</div><div class=\"answer-line\">battery should have:</div><div class=\"answer-line\">“Double ring” appearance on PA. Coins are homogeneous whereas</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">batteries have a circular lucency that appears as a double-ring or halo o</div><div class=\"answer-line\">sign</div><div class=\"answer-line\">“Step off” appearance on lateral view</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">outline other measures that can be taken to slow oesophageal injury (2</div><div class=\"answer-line\">marks)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Honey or sucralfate (crushed and mixed with 10mL water) 10mL every 10</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">minutes x 6 doses. Coats the battery and reduces hydrolysis</div><div class=\"answer-line\">In this case, the button battery has passed beyond the pylorus and the child is</div><div class=\"answer-line\">asymptomatic.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">e. Outline your discharge instructions (4 marks)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Can be discharged and observed at home for a repeat X-ray in 10 – 14 days if</div><div class=\"answer-line\">the battery has not been observed in the stool. Once the battery is beyond</div><div class=\"answer-line\">the pylorus it is very unlikely they will develop any complications however,</div><div class=\"answer-line\">give parents advice to return if: they are concerned; the child develops</div><div class=\"answer-line\">abdominal pain; bloody vomit or faeces; problems swallowing; respiratory</div><div class=\"answer-line\">distress; or a persistent cough.</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":7,"title":"","stemText":"A 75-year-old man presented to your ED with a possible stroke syndrome and had urgent CT brain plus Circle of Willis. During the scan he became acutely breathless and has been rushed back to ED with severely increased work of breathing. His airway is intact, he has a widespread wheeze in both lung fields, O2 saturations 85% on NRBM oxygen, and his BP is 80/40 mm Hg.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"What are your initial priorities? (4 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List three (3) differential diagnoses (3 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"He now has BP 70/40 mm Hg despite your interventions and is hypoxic at 83%. He is too breathless to lie flat. A decision is made to intubate the patient. Describe and justify your preparation for RSI in this particular patient (5 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">1. Resus room, form team: assign roles - TL, airway, access etc – consider</div><div class=\"answer-line\">request for anaesthetic support</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2. Presumed anaphylaxis</div><div class=\"answer-line\">0.5mg IM adrenaline and repeat if necessary.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">→</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Consider adrenaline infusion and neb salbutamol.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">3. IVF bolus 500ml crystalloid</div><div class=\"answer-line\">4. Seek and treat underlying cause – anaphylaxis to contrast vs. APO 2nd to</div><div class=\"answer-line\">stroke – needs ECG, CXR, bloods +/- POCUS</div><div class=\"answer-line\">5. Can consider early intubation given hypoxia on NRBM</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Despite your initial management, he remains shocked and breathless, and now</div><div class=\"answer-line\">describes central crushing chest pain. His 12 lead ECG shows an acute anterior</div><div class=\"answer-line\">STEMI</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Here is an indicative image from the Lung Ultrasound. Note that<strong> all</strong> this man’s lung</div><div class=\"answer-line\">fields exhibit this pattern.</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Possibilities</div><div class=\"answer-line\">- Cardiogenic shock and APO</div><div class=\"answer-line\">related to anaphylaxis plus adrenaline unmasking pre-existing coronary</div><div class=\"answer-line\">artery disease</div><div class=\"answer-line\">- Ongoing partially treated anaphylaxis</div><div class=\"answer-line\">- Type 2 ischaemic ECG changes reflecting hypoperfusion from anaphylaxis</div><div class=\"answer-line\">He now has BP 70/40 mm Hg despite your interventions and is hypoxic at 83%. He</div><div class=\"answer-line\">is too breathless to lie flat. A decision is made to intubate the patient.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Describe and justify your preparation for RSI in this particular patient</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">1. Informed verbal consent if possible (patient is still conscious): extremely</div><div class=\"answer-line\">high risk of death either way, but on balance the team considers that it’s</div><div class=\"answer-line\">safer to proceed to cardiac catheter</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2. Delegate a staff member to speak to relatives/ support person</div><div class=\"answer-line\">3. Additional resources: anaesthetics team because high risk RSI, and ICU</div><div class=\"answer-line\">because will need to take over in catheter lab.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">4. Additional monitoring and lines eg artline if time permits</div><div class=\"answer-line\">5. Optimise BP: e.g. increase IV adrenaline infusion aiming for a perfusing BP,</div><div class=\"answer-line\">and bolus dosing of adrenaline just prior to RSI</div><div class=\"answer-line\">6. Optimise oxygenation e.g. High Flow Nasal Prong Oxygen; consider a trial of</div><div class=\"answer-line\">NIV although personally I wouldn’t because of effect on preload</div><div class=\"answer-line\">7. Extreme caution with sedating agent eg as little as 10-20mg ketamine</div><div class=\"answer-line\">because of FX on BP</div><div class=\"answer-line\">8. Higher than usual dose of rocuronium eg 2mg/kg bc slowed circulation time</div><div class=\"answer-line\">9. Post-RSI ventilator settings: High rate to manage acidosis, but mitigate</div><div class=\"answer-line\">against high intra-thoracic pressures by avoiding excessive tidal volumes &amp;</div><div class=\"answer-line\">PEEP</div><div class=\"answer-line\">10.Post-RSI sedation: should you avoid traditional ‘morphine and midazolam’</div><div class=\"answer-line\">and simply use midazolam / ketamine boluses?</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":8,"title":"","stemText":"A 17-year-old female presents to ED with 2 days of pleuritic chest pain, onset during running. She is otherwise well and takes the OCP as her only medication.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List Well’s criteria for Pulmonary Embolism (7 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"Whilst in the waiting room she develops abdominal pain, vomiting and looks increasingly pale and diaphoretic. A mobile CXR is performed. List two (2) abnormalities in this CXR AND provide your interpretation. (3 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"Outline two (2) investigations AND two (2) immediate management priorities for this patient (4 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Clinical signs and symptoms of DVT</div><div class=\"answer-line\">PE is #1 diagnosis OR equally likely</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Heart rate &gt; 100</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Immobilisation at least 3 days OR surgery in the last 4 weeks</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Previous, objective diagnosed PE or DVT</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Haemoptysis</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Malignancy w/ treatment within 6 months or palliative</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Whilst in the waiting room she develops abdominal pain, vomiting and looks</div><div class=\"answer-line\">increasingly pale and diaphoretic. A mobile CXR is performed.</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Mediastinal Shift</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Large bowel loop in Lt hemithorax (with fluid level)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Interpretation: Likely diaphragmatic hernia with volvulus / bowel obstruction</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Her vitals at this point:</div><div class=\"answer-line\">HR 133</div><div class=\"answer-line\">BP 143/80</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Sats 93%</div><div class=\"answer-line\">RR 28</div><div class=\"answer-line\">GCS 15</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Outline two (2) investigations AND two (2) immediate management</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Analgesia – Parenteral (1)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Decompression - NG placement (1) + repeat CXR (1)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Exclude complications -VBG – Acidosis / Lactate rise (1)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">CT Chest acceptable (1)</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":9,"title":"","stemText":"A 23-year-old male is brought into your regional trauma centre by ambulance following a motorcycle accident where his bike has struck by an oncoming vehicle at 60km/hr. He was wearing a helmet and had no loss of consciousness. He is reporting left sided chest pain and difficulty breathing. He is diaphoretic and pale. You note a large abrasion over the left lower chest/upper abdomen.\nHis vital signs are:\n\nRR 24 per minute.\n\nHR 118 beats/minute\n\nBP 110/72 mmHg\n\nSpO2 99% (Hudson mask)\n\nGCS: E3 V5 M6 (14/15)","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List three bedside investigations you would perform in the resuscitation bay AND justify their utilisation (6 marks)."},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"He undergoes a mobile chest x-ray in the resus bay (below). List three (3) significant findings on this CXR (3 marks). *Must have BOLD to pass."},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"The patient is found to have a positive FAST scan and whilst awaiting further CT imaging, he becomes profoundly breathless, hypoxic and agitated. List six immediate management priorities for this patient (6 marks)."}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td>Investigation</td><td>Justification</td></tr><tr><td>POCUS/eFAST</td><td>?Pneumo- or haemothorax. ?Intraperitoneal</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">free fluid</td></tr><tr><td>Venous blood</td><td>Lactate / Acid-base / Hb / pCO2 (?adequate</td></tr><tr><td>gas</td><td>ventilation)</td></tr><tr><td>12-lead ECG</td><td>?new ECG changes suggesting myocardial</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">contusion</td></tr><tr><td>BSL</td><td>Exclude concomitant hypoglycaemia</td></tr><tr><td>ROTEM / TEG</td><td>Assess for acute traumatic coagulopathy</td></tr><tr><td>Blood alcohol</td><td>Forensic requirement</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">He is reporting left sided chest pain and difficulty breathing. He is diaphoretic and</div><div class=\"answer-line\">pale. You note a large abrasion over the left lower chest/upper abdomen.</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">His current vital signs are;</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Respiratory rate 40 per minute.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Pulse 142 beats/minute</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Blood pressure 88/60 mmHg</div><div class=\"answer-line\">SpO2 81% (Non-rebreather mask)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">c. List six immediate management priorities for this patient (6 marks).</div><div class=\"answer-line\">1. Immediate pleural decompression for suspected tension pneumothorax +/-</div><div class=\"answer-line\">haemothorax</div><div class=\"answer-line\">a. Ultimately requires formal intercostal drain</div><div class=\"answer-line\">b. Low-dose sedation / Local anaesthetic / Triangle of safety</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2. Optimise oxygenation</div><div class=\"answer-line\">a. NIV/CPAP (100% O2, PEEP 5-&gt;8-&gt;10)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">3. Commence blood transfusion</div><div class=\"answer-line\">a. 1:1:1 ratio (RBC:FFP:PLT)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">b. Target SBP~90 &amp; increased perfusion</div><div class=\"answer-line\">4. Detect &amp; correct coagulopathy</div><div class=\"answer-line\">5. Tranexamic acid 1g slow IV push</div><div class=\"answer-line\">6. Rapid sequence intubation</div><div class=\"answer-line\">a. Indication: Respiratory failure &amp; expected clinical course</div><div class=\"answer-line\">b. Pre-ox with PEEP</div><div class=\"answer-line\">c. Reduced dose ketamine (elevated shock index)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">d. 1.5-2mg/kg rocuronium</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":10,"title":"","stemText":"A normally well 18-month-old girl is brought into your ED by her parents. She has been unwell for 1 week with an URTI and fever. 3 days ago, she developed a rash which started on her face and torso. The rash now involves both arms and legs and perineum. The parents have been applying wet dressings and topical steroid as per the GP for a flare of her normally mild eczema. The patient appears coryzal and miserable on first impression.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List three (3) features of the rash (3 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"State the most likely diagnosis (1 mark)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"Describe three (3) investigations that you would perform in this child and explain the rationale behind each (3 marks)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"State six (6) key components of your management of the patient. (6 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">monomorphic blisters/vesicles filed with clear yellow/thick purulent fluid/material</div><div class=\"answer-line\">some new blisters with umbilication</div><div class=\"answer-line\">some older blisters with crusting</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Some blood-stained blisters (red/purple/black)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Some secondary bacterial infection with staph or strep resulting in impetigo</div><div class=\"answer-line\">additionally</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Eczema Herpeticum (considered one of the few dermatological emergencies) can</div><div class=\"answer-line\">be diagnosed clinically when a patient with known atopic dermatitis presents with</div><div class=\"answer-line\">an acute eruption of</div><div class=\"answer-line\">painful, monomorphic clustered vesicles associated with fever and malaise</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">.1 Viral swab for culture/HSV PCR taken by scraping the base of a fresh blister to</div><div class=\"answer-line\">confirm clinical diagnosis</div><div class=\"answer-line\">.2 Bacterial swab for microscopy and culture as eczema herpeticum can be</div><div class=\"answer-line\">complicated by secondary bacterial infection</div><div class=\"answer-line\">.3Slit lamp eye exam/fluoroscene to rule out ulcer/corneal involvement.</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">marks)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">admit to hospital under Paediatrics/consult dermatology service</div><div class=\"answer-line\">IV acyclovir 50mg m2 dose</div><div class=\"answer-line\">IV Flucloxacillin 50mg/kg dose 8hourly</div><div class=\"answer-line\">soak to remove bacterial crusts/ topical emollient and wet dressings</div><div class=\"answer-line\">analgesia regular panadol and neurofen. IN fentanyl for wet dressings</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Urgent ophthalmology review if eye involvement</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":11,"title":"","stemText":"A 6-year-old boy presents with a non-blanching rash (photo below), sore throat and leg pain. Your intern thinks it is Henoch - Schonlein Purpura (HSP).","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List three (3) other differential diagnoses important to consider (3 marks):"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List three (3) key clinical features required to diagnose HSP? (3 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"List three (3) complications of HSP? (3 marks)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"Outline your management of HSP in this child in the ED (5 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Bacterial disease:</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Meningococcal disease</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Pneumococcal disease</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Other bacterial – Haemophilus, Group A strep, Staph aureus</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Viral disease – entero, adeno, influenza</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Haematological – ITP, malignancy eg acute leukaemia, aplastic anaemia,</div><div class=\"answer-line\">DIC, HUS, drug induced TCP</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Other Vasculitis</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Rash + one or more of…</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Arthritis/arthralgia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Abdominal pain</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Nephritis (UA sig proteinuria/macroscopic haematuria/HT)</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">a. Renal – HT, nephritis, GN, Nephrotic Syn</div><div class=\"answer-line\">b. GI – Intussusception/GI bleeding/Bowel</div><div class=\"answer-line\">infarction/perforation/pancreatitis</div><div class=\"answer-line\">c. Genital – testicular pain/orchitis/necrosis</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"2\">e. Respiratory (rare) diffuse alveolar haemorrhage</td></tr><tr><td>f.</td><td>Neuro (rare) – mood change, apathy, hyperactivity, encephalopathy,</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">ICH</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">d. Outline your management of HSP in this child in the ED (5 marks)</td></tr><tr><td>i.</td><td>Symptom management</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">- Bed rest and elevation of LL</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">- Regular paracetamol 20mg/kg QID PO</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">- Short course NSAIDs eg. Ibuprofen 10mg /kg TDS</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">- Consider Steroids for his mod – severe joint pain: pred 1-2mg/kg daily</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Whilst symptoms persist.</td></tr><tr><td>ii.</td><td>Management of mod-severe HSP and any specific complications</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Note, steroids do not impact long term prognosis or renal</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">complications (mainly for treatment of joint/testicular/abdo pain)</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Immunoglobulin also contentious - supportive management</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Discuss with Paediatric team and relevant specialty team re</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">complications eg. Renal, surgical</td></tr><tr><td>iii.</td><td>Organise disposition</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">- Mild – GP followup vs Mod-severe Paeds follow up or admission</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">- Follow up for repeat urines and BP - HITH/clinic/GP</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">- Advice to parents – analgesia ladder, look out for complications eg.</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Haematuria/oedema/bowel obstruction</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">- Factsheet</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":12,"title":"","stemText":"A 65-year-old male presents to the emergency department with confusion and generalized weakness. His past medical history includes hypertension, diabetes and congestive heart failure. On examination, he is unkempt, lethargic, with a blood pressure of 130/80 mmHg, heart rate of 100 bpm, and respiratory rate of 18 breaths per minute. Laboratory investigations reveal a serum sodium level of 120 mmol/L.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Complete the following table relating to possible causes of this man’s low sodium with two (2) causes in each column: (6 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List four (4) investigations you would order for this man to help determine the cause of his hyponatremia and justify your choice (4 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"He has a generalised tonic- clonic seizure. List 3 intravenous therapies from 3 different classes that you would consider giving to treat his seizure. Include doses in your answer. (3 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td>Hypervolae</td><td>Euvolaemic</td><td>Hypovolaemic</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">mic</td></tr><tr><td>CCF</td><td>Intake related- “tea &amp;</td><td>Diuretics</td></tr><tr><td>Cirrhosis</td><td>toast” / beer diet</td><td>Osmotic diuresis with poorly</td></tr><tr><td>Renal</td><td>Water intoxication</td><td>controlled diabetes</td></tr><tr><td>disease</td><td>SIADH due to pneumonia /</td><td>Diarrhoea/Sweat/vomiting</td></tr><tr><td>malignancy etc</td><td>Burns / heat exposure</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td>Investigation</td><td>Justification</td></tr><tr><td>BSL</td><td>Exclude pseudohyponatremia due to</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">hyperglycaemia</td></tr><tr><td>Urine &amp; serum</td><td>Support diagnosis SIADH if urine &gt; serum</td></tr><tr><td>osmolarity</td><td>osm</td></tr><tr><td>CXR</td><td>CCF, evidence pneumonia or lung</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">malignancy</td></tr><tr><td>CTB</td><td>Exclude ICH / cerebral malignancy</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">He has a generalised tonic- clonic seizure.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">1. Midazolam 5mg</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2. 3% NaCl 100mL</div><div class=\"answer-line\">3. 50% dextrose 25-50mL</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":13,"title":"","stemText":"A 50-year-old female presents to your emergency department with 5 days of isolated fever. She returned from a week’s holiday in Bali 7 days ago. She has no past medical history, no allergies and takes no regular medications.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Other than the information given above, list five (5) key questions to ask in your history (5 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"The patient is confirmed to have Typhoid fever. Other than Salmonella Typhi, list five (5) pathogens to consider in the clinical case (5 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"What is the initial treatment of confirmed Typhoid fever? (2 marks)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"List two (2) potential complications of Typhoid fever (2 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Symptoms – diarrhoea, constipation, cough, rose spots on chest,</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">spontaneous bleeding,</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Travel location and type (country/regions, urban/rural, living conditions,</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">altitude)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Exposure to risk factors</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">contact with animals, insect bites</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">drinking water and food sources</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">activities (including sexual contacts, illicit drug use, tattoos)</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">sick contacts</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Immunisation history (routine childhood and travel specific,</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">noting Salmonella typhi vaccination only has ~70% efficacy)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Prophylaxis</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">medication use and adherence</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">mosquito nets/insect repellent</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Healthcare while away including any medications taken</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">The patient is confirmed to have Typhoid fever.</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">[Do not accept typhoid fever, enteric fever, salmonella or paratyphoid]</div><div class=\"answer-line\">HIV seroconversion</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Dengue</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Malaria</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Arbovirus: Chickungunya, zika</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Ross river virus</div><div class=\"answer-line\">COVID</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Influenza</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Non travel related infecsions and Sepsis (max 1 mark)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Hepatitis</div><div class=\"answer-line\">PID</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Q Fever</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Shigella</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Measles</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Ricketsea</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">azithromycin 1 g (child: 20 mg/kg up to 1 g) orally, daily for 5 to 7 days</div><div class=\"answer-line\">OR (if susceptibility is confirmed)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">1ciprofloxacin 500 mg (child: 12.5 mg/kg up to 500 mg) orally, 12-hourly for 7</div><div class=\"answer-line\">days [Note 16] [Note 17].</div><div class=\"answer-line\">In adults and children older than 1 year with severe typhoid or paratyphoid fever</div><div class=\"answer-line\">or who are unable to tolerate oral therapy, use intravenous therapy initially:</div><div class=\"answer-line\">1azithromycin  500 mg (child: 10 mg/kg up to 500 mg) intravenously, daily until</div><div class=\"answer-line\">oral azithromycin can be tolerated (total duration of 5 to 7 days [intravenous +</div><div class=\"answer-line\">oral])</div><div class=\"answer-line\">OR (if susceptibility is confirmed)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">1ciprofloxacin 400 mg (child: 10 mg/kg up to 400 mg) intravenously, 12-hourly until</div><div class=\"answer-line\">oral ciprofloxacin can be tolerated (total duration of 7 days [intravenous +</div><div class=\"answer-line\">oral]) [Note 16]</div><div class=\"answer-line\">OR</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2ceftriaxone 2 g (child 1 year or older: 100 mg/kg up to 2 g) intravenously, daily;</div><div class=\"answer-line\">for patients with septic shock or requiring intensive care support, use 1 g (child 1</div><div class=\"answer-line\">month or older: 50 mg/kg up to 1 g) intravenously, 12-hourly. When an adequate</div><div class=\"answer-line\">clinical response is achieved and oral therapy can be tolerated, switch to oral</div><div class=\"answer-line\">azithromycin or ciprofloxacin, depending on results of susceptibility testing (total</div><div class=\"answer-line\">duration at least 7 days [intravenous + oral]).</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Bowel Perforation</div><div class=\"answer-line\">DIC /Sepsis /Pneumonia /pancreatitis / encephalitis/hepatitis/Renal failure /Chronic</div><div class=\"answer-line\">carrier state / Myocarditis /Cholecystitis /Small bowel ulceration</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":14,"title":"","stemText":"A 6-month-old male is brought into ED by his parents with intermittent abdominal pain, vomiting, lethargy and an episode of passing red currant jelly stools. Your working diagnosis is intussusception.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List five (5) additional features of patient history that would increase the likelihood of intussusception. (5 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"State the shape and location of the abdominal mass that would be suggestive of intussusception. (2 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"List two (2) radiological findings on AXR that suggest intussusception. (2 marks)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"Aside from analgesia and keeping the patient nil by mouth, list three (3) important steps in the management of this patient. (3 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Answer:</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Pallor during episodes of abdominal pain/distress</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Past history of intussusception</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Recent bowel surgery</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Recent rotavirus vaccination</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Recent adenovirus infection</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">History of condition acting as potential lead point:</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Meckel’s diverticulum</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Henoch Schonlein Purpura</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Lymphoma</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Luminal polyps (Peutz Jegher Syndrome)</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">The child is currently settled between episodes and able to be examined.</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Answer:</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Sausage-shaped mass</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Located in right abdomen, crossing midline in epigastrium or behind</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">umbilicus</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Ultrasound services are not available at your hospital. You elect to perform an</div><div class=\"answer-line\">abdominal x-ray (AXR).</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Answer:</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Abnormal gas pattern with empty RLQ and visible soft tissue mass in</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">upper abdomen</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Crescent sign: soft tissue mass with crescent lucency of bowel gas</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Lack of faecal material in large bowel</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">In addition to signs consistent with intussusception, the patient’s AXR has findings</div><div class=\"answer-line\">of bowel perforation.</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Answer:</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Nasogastric tube insertion (free drainage)</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">IV antibiotics (amoxicillin, gentamicin, metronidazole)</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Urgent surgical review</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">NOT contrast/gas enema: contraindicated if perforation present</div><div class=\"answer-line\">(Zero marks for this part if lists contrast/gas enema)</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":15,"title":"","stemText":"A 56-year-old male presents to triage complaining of acute pain in his right eye.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"What 3 risk factors would you enquire about when considering whether this patient has keratitis? (3 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"What features on history help distinguish between episcleritis and scleritis? (6 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"On examination, a 1.5mm dendritic lesion on the cornea is noted. This lesion enhances to fluorescein and is inferonasal. Visual acuity is 6/6. What is the diagnosis? (1 mark)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"Outline your management plan. (3 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Answer: immunocompromised, contact lens wearer, diabetic, other appropriate</div><div class=\"answer-line\">risk factor</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td>Episcleritis</td><td>Scleritis</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Associated</td><td>idiopathic</td><td>Associated with RA, autoimmune disease</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">conditions</td></tr><tr><td>Symptoms</td><td>Acute onset, mild</td><td>Subacute onset, severe pain, pain with eye</td></tr><tr><td>pain, redness,</td><td>movement, blurred vision or vision loss,</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>irritation</td><td>photophobia</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Slit lamp</td><td>Mobile vessels,</td><td>Adherent vessels, red/blue discolouration</td></tr><tr><td>exam</td><td>reddish hue</td><td></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">On examination, a 1.5mm dendritic lesion on the cornea is noted. This lesion</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">enhances to fluorescein and is inferonasal. Visual acuity is 6/6.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td>Associated</td><td>idiopathic</td><td>Associated with RA, autoimmune disease</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">conditions</td></tr><tr><td>Symptoms</td><td>Acute onset, mild</td><td>Subacute onset, severe pain, pain with eye</td></tr><tr><td>pain, redness,</td><td>movement, blurred vision or vision loss,</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>irritation</td><td>photophobia</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Slit lamp</td><td>Mobile vessels,</td><td>Adherent vessels, red/blue discolouration</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">reddish hue</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">PO antiviral eg acyclovir 800mg 5x daily or valaciclovir 1g TDS</div><div class=\"answer-line\">urgent ophthalmological referral.     2 marks</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":16,"title":"","stemText":"You are working in a large tertiary Emergency Department. You received an ambulance notification for an incoming patient. A 23-year-old female has been found with reduced level of consciousness.\n\nHR 130bpm\nUnrecordable BP Unrecordable saturations Deeply cyanosed Her family notes she has been feeling short of breath recently. She has no past medical history of note. She is on the oral contraceptive pill. Family do not think she takes any other drugs or has any allergies.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Other than PE, list five (5) differential diagnosis that you would prepare for. (5 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List three (3) features on echo that would suggest a PE (3 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"List three (3) indications for thrombolysis in PE (3 marks)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"You have determined that this patient has a massive PE. Despite fluid resuscitation she remains hypotensive, she is very cold and clammy and is less alert than upon arrival. She is unable to receive thrombolysis due to a known structural cerebral vascular lesion. List three (3) other medical interventions could you consider in the management of this unstable patient (3 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Severe sepsis / pneumonia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Trauma – HI</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Overdose of medication – BZD / alcohol</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Seizure</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Pregnancy – ectopic, intra-abdominal bleeding</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Cardiac anything – arrhythmia from underlying cardiac disease – ARVD/</div><div class=\"answer-line\">HOCM</div><div class=\"answer-line\">When she arrives, she is conscious – she feels very cold, she is cyanosed and has</div><div class=\"answer-line\">difficult to palpate peripheral pulses.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">BP 75/45</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Sats 80% RA, improve to 92% NRM</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Temp 34.9</div><div class=\"answer-line\">HR 133bpm – sinus tachycardia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Bedside echo is performed.</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">1. Septal flattening with RV pressure overload – ‘D sign’</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2. Severely dilated RV with reduced systolic function</div><div class=\"answer-line\">3. McConnell sign</div><div class=\"answer-line\">4. Dilated IVC with lack of respiratory collapse</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Cardiac arrest</div><div class=\"answer-line\">SBP &lt;90 for 15 mins or persistent bradycardia &lt;40 with clinical features of</div><div class=\"answer-line\">shock or requiring vasopressors to maintain BP</div><div class=\"answer-line\">High risk sub-massive PE – See flow chart below</div><div class=\"answer-line\">You have determined that this patient has a massive PE. Despite fluid resuscitation</div><div class=\"answer-line\">she remains hypotensive, she is very cold and clammy and is less alert than upon</div><div class=\"answer-line\">arrival.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">She is unable to receive thrombolysis due to a known structural cerebral vascular</div><div class=\"answer-line\">lesion.</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">1. Commence vasopressors for haemodynamic support –Adr/ Nad infusion /</div><div class=\"answer-line\">vasopressin infusion.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">(dobutamine / milrinone – to be used with vasopressor as can cause</div><div class=\"answer-line\">vasodilation – not great with systemic hypotension)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2. Consider nebulised GTN to attempt to reduced pulmonary hypertension / RV</div><div class=\"answer-line\">strain (pulmonary vasodilator)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">3. Interventional radiology clot extraction</div><div class=\"answer-line\">4. Cardiothoracic surgical extraction</div><div class=\"answer-line\">5. VA ECMO</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":17,"title":"","stemText":"A 2-week-old neonate is brought to your outer urban district hospital ED by his mother with a 8-hour-long history of distress and several episodes of “yellow-green vomit”. He looks floppy, pale and lethargic. You are most concerned about intestinal malrotation with midgut volvulus.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List five (5) other important causes of bilious vomiting in a neonate (5 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"Discuss the imaging modalities available to evaluate for midgut volvulus (3 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"What are your immediate management priorities? (4 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">marks)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Malrotation with midgut volvulus</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Intestinal atresia (duod/jej-ileal/colon)</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Meconium ileus</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Necrotising enterocolitis</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Intestinal perforation</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Hirschsprungs</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Anorectal malformation</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Septic ileus</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">GORD</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Gastric dysmotility</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">AXR - rarely helpful. Frequently normal or non-diagnostic. May have mild</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">dilation, gasless abdomen, double-bubble sign</div><div class=\"answer-line\">USS - often used as an initial screening tool - if diagnostic</div><div class=\"answer-line\">laparotomy. If</div><div class=\"answer-line\">→</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">not diagnostic need contrast imaging</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Upper GI contrast series - with contrast swallow</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Abdo CT - less sens/spec than upper GI series</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">IV access/check sugar/fluid resuscitation</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">IV antibiotics to cover intra-abdominal source +/- empiric sepsis cover</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Urgent surgical involvement - if Dx confirmed needs urgent laparotomy</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Early contact with neonatal transport service - likely to need urgent TF to</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">tertiary paeds</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Looking after the parents</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":18,"title":"","stemText":"A 29-year-old female has taken an overdose of citalopram (40 x citalopram tablets 40mg each) 4 hours ago. She is alert, anxious and has HR 115 bpm BP 120/83 with no other examination findings of significance. An ECG is taken.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Describe and interpret the ECG (4 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"In general, when a long QT is found, we use formulae or calculators to determine a “corrected” QT interval. What is the purpose of “correcting” the QT? (2 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"In acute poisonings with QT-prolonging medications, we generally use a QT nomogram (not Bezett’s or similar) - why? (3 marks)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"How should one measure the QT to plot it on the nomogram? (3 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Tachy – rate 110-115 – probably sinus tachy with subtle P-waves indenting terminal</div><div class=\"answer-line\">part of the T-waves – junctional tachy would be acceptable answer. Non-specific</div><div class=\"answer-line\">IVCD QRSD 120 Prolonged QT (400 msec) Prominent R-wave in aVR</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Consistent with sodium channel blockade from citalopram with long QT and risk of</div><div class=\"answer-line\">TdP</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">QT intervals normally shorten with tachycardia and lengthen with bradycardia.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">“Correcting” applies a formula (say Bazett’s) to factor in the rate or the RR interval</div><div class=\"answer-line\">to estimate what the QT would be at 60 bpm. This allows comparison of the</div><div class=\"answer-line\">patient’s QT over time or, for example, with the addition of a medication.</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">In toxicological causes of prolonged QT, the risk of torsades is better determined</div><div class=\"answer-line\">by the absolute QT interval (not corrected to HR = 60) and the actual HR (at the</div><div class=\"answer-line\">time the QT is measured) noting that tachycardia (common in many overdoses) is</div><div class=\"answer-line\">relatively protective against torsades. Most torsades occurs when the patient</div><div class=\"answer-line\">becomes relatively bradycardic.</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">The QT interval should be measured manually on a 12-lead ECG from the</div><div class=\"answer-line\">beginning of the Q wave until to the end of the T wave in multiple leads (i.e. six</div><div class=\"answer-line\">leads including limb and chest leads and median QT calculated).</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":19,"title":"","stemText":"An 18-month-old boy is brought to ED with a painful swollen thigh after an unwitnessed fall at day-care. He weighs 12 kg.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List two (2) abnormalities shown in these x-rays (2 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part B","prompt":"The child has had paracetamol and ibuprofen at triage. Complete the following table with details of 3 further pharmacological options for pain relief (6 marks)"},{"id":"e","label":"Part C","prompt":"List two (2) fractures which would be concerning for Non-Accidental Injury (NAI) in this age group (2 marks)"},{"id":"f","label":"Part D","prompt":"List three (3) other features which would be concerning for NAI. (3 marks)"}],"propPages":["asset:debug_2024_ns_q19_xray_ap","asset:debug_2024_ns_q19_xray_lateral"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"removedNonQuestionResponseFragments":["0.5 marks per feature","1 mark for drug, half mark for each of route and dose"],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Spiral fracture of the <strong>femoral shaft</strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Mid-shaft / junction of the proximal and middle thirds</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Angulated</div><div class=\"answer-marking-note\">0.5 marks per feature. The words <strong>femur</strong> and <strong>shaft</strong> are required for full marks.</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><thead><tr><th>Drug</th><th>Route</th><th>Dose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fentanyl</td><td>Intranasal</td><td>18 micrograms (accept 12–24 micrograms)</td></tr><tr><td>Oxycodone</td><td>Oral</td><td>1.2 mg</td></tr><tr><td>Morphine</td><td>Intravenous</td><td>1.2 mg</td></tr><tr><td>Ropivacaine</td><td>Regional block</td><td>30–36 mg</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"answer-marking-note\">One mark for the drug and half a mark each for route and dose. Any three appropriate options are required.</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Rib fracture</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Classic metaphyseal lesion</strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Complex skull fracture</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Humeral fracture with epiphyseal separation attributed to a fall of less than 1 metre</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Delayed attendance (&gt;24 hours in a child with obvious distress)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">History of impact from a toy or other object causing a fracture</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Other injuries on assessment that are not related to the fracture</div><div class=\"answer-line\">No trauma history to explain the fracture</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Mechanism inconsistent with the developmental stage of the child</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":20,"title":"","stemText":"You are an emergency physician in a tertiary teaching hospital mixed Emergency department. You see James, a 22-month-old boy, who presents with his parents with generalised scrotal swelling, erythema and associated distress. In the last 1 month, he has had one similar presentation at which testicular torsion was excluded during scrotal exploration, an atraumatic limp due to left leg discomfort (with normal imaging and blood investigations), and a toddler’s right femoral fracture. He was previously entirely fit and well. He takes no regular medications, has no known allergies, and is immunised to date for his age. He is growing and developing well. On examination, he weighs 15kg, has stable normal vital signs, has no respiratory distress, is warm and well-perfused, and has a scant but generalised petechial rash. His temperature is 38.3. He is otherwise difficult to examine due to distress. You perform blood tests - results are reproduced below: Hb 62 g/L WCC 64 x 10^9/L (lymphocytes 63 x 10^9/L, neutrophils 0.4 x 10^9/L)\n\nPlatelets 19 x 10^9/L INR 1.3, PT 14.4 seconds, APTT 31 seconds, Fibrinogen 2.5 g/L LDH 540 U/L, Urate 0.17 mmol/L Blood group: A Positive. Normal BGL, VBG, EUC, CMP, LFTs, Lipase.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List three (3) additional investigations you would request (3 marks)."},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"Blood film - assess for presence of blasts Blood culture - in context of febrile immunosuppressed state State three (3) causes of acute deterioration (over the first minutes to hours of presentation) for which this patient is at risk. (3 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"Describe your management priorities for this patient. (6 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Must include blood film for 1 mark, and any other 2 points.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Blood film – assess for presence of blasts</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Flow cytometry – identify leukaemia type</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Blood culture – in context of febrile immunosuppressed state</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Chest x-ray – assess for mediastinal mass</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Urine culture – in context of febrile immunosuppressed state</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Scrotal US – ensure vascularity</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">b. State three (3) causes of acute deterioration (over the first minutes to</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Any three of the points below</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Acute ischaemic stroke secondary to hyperviscosity syndrome</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Tumour lysis syndrome leading to electrolyte derangement and arrhythmia,</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">or acute renal failure.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Septic shock</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Disseminated intravascular coagulation with multi-organ dysfunction</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Haemorrhage secondary to thrombocytopenia</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Progressive anaemia with high cardiac output cardiac failure</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Oncology/Haematology consult for 1 mark, breaking bad news for 1 mark,</div><div class=\"answer-line\">then any other 4 points.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Urgent Paediatric Oncology consultation (reasonable to accept Paediatric</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Haematology consultation as alternative) - guide investigation,</div><div class=\"answer-line\">management, disposition</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Social worker urgent referral – family support during notification of</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">diagnosis and treatment</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Breaking bad news discussion with parents – concern for leukaemia,</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">prognosis and treatment plan unclear (pending further tests), plan for</div><div class=\"answer-line\">expedited Oncology review, offer and check support structures.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Arrange transfer to a tertiary paediatric hospital for Paediatric Oncology</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">admission</div><div class=\"answer-line\">IV fluids</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">o Resuscitation fluids not indicated.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">o Full maintenance rate fluids maintenance fluids – 50ml/hr N/Saline + 5%</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Dextrose, no added potassium (given risk of tumour lysis syndrome and</div><div class=\"answer-line\">hyperkalaemia).</div><div class=\"answer-line\">o Practice differs between paediatric centres re: hydration strategy</div><div class=\"answer-line\">(maintaining euvolaemia, vs. over-hydration). Therefore can also accept</div><div class=\"answer-line\">double-maintenance rate (100ml/hr) fluids, provide rationale given (over-</div><div class=\"answer-line\">hydration to reduce risk of hyperviscosity syndrome and ischaemic stroke).</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Allopurinol 10mg/kg/day in 3 divided doses</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Broad-spectrum empiric antibiotics for febrile immunosuppressed patient</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">and ddx of serious bacterial infection.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">o Tazocin 100mg/kg IV q6h as single agent is reasonable.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">o Add Gentamicin 7.5mg/kg IV stat dose if features of severe sepsis or septic</div><div class=\"answer-line\">shock</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Analgesia</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">o Paracetamol 15mg/kg = 225mg per dose, orally, q6h</div><div class=\"answer-line\">o Pre-IV access: Fentanyl 1.5mcg/kg = 22.5mcg/dose, intranasal, STAT, for</div><div class=\"answer-line\">severe pain. o Oxycodone 0.1mg/kg = 1.5mg per dose, PO, q4h; OR</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Morphine 0.1mg/kg = 1.5mg, IV, q2h (max 6mg/24h); OR Fentanyl 1mcg/kg</div><div class=\"answer-line\">= 15mcg/dose, IV, q15mins (max 60mcg/24h)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">o No NSAIDs (thrombocytopenia, haemorrhage risk)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Transfusion approach</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">o Consider platelet transfusion in discussion with Oncology/Haematology –</div><div class=\"answer-line\">reasonable to recommend a 15ml/kg transfusion, although no urgent</div><div class=\"answer-line\">trigger for this apart from the platelet count (would urgently consider if</div><div class=\"answer-line\">haemorrhage, or if platelet count &lt; 10).</div><div class=\"answer-line\">o No urgent indication for PRBC – reconsider if symptomatic anaemia or Hb</div><div class=\"answer-line\">&lt; 60 (given risk of PRBC lysis exacerbating tumour lysis syndrome)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Plan for serial bloods – trend of Hb and Platelet count, tumour lysis</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">syndrome screen, DIC screen</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Fasting plan (if bone marrow aspirate expected).</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":21,"title":"","stemText":"Marc an 18-month-old boy is brought in by mum to his local non major referral centre after pulling a boiling bowl of noodles in hot water out of the microwave 1 ago, burning his whole face, neck and chest/back and right arm. He is normally well. Mum rushed him straight to the ED by car.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Estimate the burn as a percentage of TBSA including depth. Include method of estimating burn surface area (4 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"Calculate and describe your fluid management for this patient (3 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"Other than IV fluids, list eight (8) management priorities for this child (8 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Difficult to assess burn depth early post injury in first 48-72 hours. This appears to</div><div class=\"answer-line\">be a mid to deep dermal burn (Better to describe if able to do CRT). Estimate size</div><div class=\"answer-line\">using Lund and Browder chart or palmar surface of hand estimate 1% TBSA.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Estimated size 9% arm, 18% front, 18%back, face 18% approx. 50%</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">marks)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Use modified Parkland formula, need to include calculation. Hartmanns is first</div><div class=\"answer-line\">choice or 0.9% saline if unavailable.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Warm fluid-pts get v hypothermic</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">3 x TBSA burn x pt weight = vol fluid required in first 24 hours.</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Vol /(8-time since burn) = mls/hr</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Adjust rate according to UO and fluid status</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Initial analgesia-IN fentanyl with dose/Dissociative dose ketamine 0.5mg/kg/IV</div><div class=\"answer-line\">morphine and dose to tolerate any procedures/dressings</div><div class=\"answer-line\">· First aid 20mins cold water compresses or irrigaton with giving set/cool</div><div class=\"answer-line\">bath/shower</div><div class=\"answer-line\">· IV/IO access-bloods HB, EUC, VBG, Glucose, G and H</div><div class=\"answer-line\">· Remove any wet clothes after first aid/cooling/ cover and dress wounds after</div><div class=\"answer-line\">initial exposure/assessment-warm room and warm fluids. Take photos with</div><div class=\"answer-line\">parental consent for Burns referral.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">· Dressings paraffin to face-cling wrap initially with multilayer bactigras and</div><div class=\"answer-line\">velband body and neck (clean burn and surrounding area with normal saline, local</div><div class=\"answer-line\">debridement loose skin, deroof blisters crossing joints.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">· This child will need intubation (must include this) for pain relief, airway</div><div class=\"answer-line\">protection-face and neck burn/ transfer to burn centre for debridement in OT and</div><div class=\"answer-line\">PICU. Ngt Post intubation. Nurse head up to reduce oedema</div><div class=\"answer-line\">· IDC aim UO 1ML/KG/HR</div><div class=\"answer-line\">· Transfer retrieval to tertiary paeds centre, PICU, burns Specialist</div><div class=\"answer-line\">· SW inv</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":22,"title":"","stemText":"A 5-year-old boy (20kg) is bought into your urban district Emergency Department by his parents with increased drowsiness following multiple episodes of vomiting. They report a previously well child without comorbidities who has had 24 hours of low-grade fever and a runny nose. You notice that he is very somnolent and is unable to follow commands.\nHis vital signs are;\n\nGCS: E3 V4 M5 (12/15)\nPulse 70 beats/minute\nBlood pressure 142/90 mmHg\n\nSpO2 97%.\nTemperature 37.8*C","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List three (3) non-infectious causes for this patient clinical presentation (3 marks)."},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"He undergoes a non-contrast CT Brain scan (below). List three (3) significant findings on this scan (3 marks). *Must have BOLD to pass."},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"The patient has a three-minute generalised tonic-clonic seizure following which they remain unconscious and are found to have a bilateral, non-reactive dilated pupils. List six (6) immediate management priorities for this patient (6 marks)."}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">1. Raised intracranial pressure (traumatic or non-traumatic)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2. Hypoglycaemia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">3. Post-ictal</div><div class=\"answer-line\">4. DKA</div><div class=\"answer-line\">5. Intussusception</div><div class=\"answer-line\">6. Non-accidental injury</div><div class=\"answer-line\">7. Intoxication / Ingestion</div><div class=\"answer-line\">He undergoes a non-contrast CT Brain scan (below).</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">You notice that he is very somnolent and is unable to follow commands.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">His vital signs are;</div><div class=\"answer-line\">GCS: E3 V4 M5 (12/15)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Pulse 70 beats/minute</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Blood pressure 142/90 mmHg</div><div class=\"answer-line\">SpO2 97%.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Temperature 37.8*C</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">marks).</div><div class=\"answer-line\">1. Undertake rapid sequence intubation for neuroprotection, airway protection</div><div class=\"answer-line\">and expected clinical course</div><div class=\"answer-line\">a. Induction: Ketamine 1.5-2mg/kg, Rocuroncium 1.5-2mg/kg</div><div class=\"answer-line\">b. Tape ETT in-situ</div><div class=\"answer-line\">c. Post-intubation sedation:</div><div class=\"answer-line\">i. Midazolam + opiate (fentanyl/morphine)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2. Hyperventilate to pCO2 ~35 (ETCO2 ~30)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">3. Head up 30*</div><div class=\"answer-line\">4. Osmotherapy:</div><div class=\"answer-line\">a. Mannitol: 0.5-1g/kg intravenously</div><div class=\"answer-line\">b. Hypertonic saline: 1-3mL/kg of 3% solution (iv)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">5. Antiepileptic drugs:</div><div class=\"answer-line\">a. Levetiracetam 20-40mg/kg</div><div class=\"answer-line\">b. Valproate 20mg/kg</div><div class=\"answer-line\">c. Phenytoin 20mg/kg</div><div class=\"answer-line\">6. IDC</div><div class=\"answer-line\">7. Detect and correct hypoglycaemia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">8. Arrange paediatric critical care retrieval to neurosurgical centre for urgent</div><div class=\"answer-line\">ventricular drain</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":23,"title":"","stemText":"A 32-year-old woman presents with a headache 9 days postpartum. She had been diagnosed with sinusitis by her GP but the headache had worsened and is diffuse and throbbing. No focal neurological findings Afebrile\n\nHR 85 bpm\n\nBP 145/95\nA non-contrast head CT is performed and is reported as normal.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"OTHER than eclampsia/pre-eclampsia, list six (6) serious causes of headache in this post-partum context that can have a normal non-contrast CT brain (6 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"What criteria must be present to diagnose post-partum preeclampsia? (3 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"The patient has a presumed eclamptic seizure. Outline four (4) immediate actions (4 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">headache in this post-partum context that can have a normal non-</div><div class=\"answer-line\">contrast CT brain (6 marks)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Pre-eclampsia/eclampsia</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndrome (RCVS)</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome (PRES)</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Cerebral venous thrombosis (including cavernous sinus thrombosis)</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Pituitary apoplexy</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Post dural puncture headache</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Idiopathic intracranial hypertension</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Carotid artery dissection/vertebral artery dissection</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Space occupying lesion (mass)</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Subarachnoid hemorrhage</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Meningitis/encephalitis</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Up to 6 weeks post-partum. BP &gt; 160/110 for two readings plus at least one of</div><div class=\"answer-line\">proteinuria, thrombocytopaenia, elevated creatinine or transaminases, APO/CCF or</div><div class=\"answer-line\">neurologic symptoms</div><div class=\"answer-line\">The patient has a presumed eclamptic seizure.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Outline four (4) immediate actions (4 marks)</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Resuscitation - ensure patent airway, O2, IV access</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Terminate seizure – give appropriate benzodiazepine (e.g. midazolam 5-</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">10mg IV)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Prevent further seizures – magnesium 4g load then 1-2g/h</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Control hypertension – e.g. IV labetalol 20-80mg IV, IV hydralazine 10mg IV.</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">MgSO4 controls seizures but not HT</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Deliver the baby</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":24,"title":"","stemText":"A 24-year-old male is airlifted to your tertiary emergency department intubated following a pedestrian vs car. He reportedly had an argument with his girlfriend while intoxicated then ran into traffic. He had no movement of arms or legs at the scene and was noted to have priapism. He has been sedated, paralysed and intubated prior to transfer. He has the following vital signs: HR 70, BP 130/80, Sats 100% on FiO2 40%, RR 20 (set by ventilator).","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"What are your initial assessment priorities? (3 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"You do not identify any additional injuries. What initial investigations will you perform? (3 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"Bloods - Hb, BAC, VBG/CO2 most important specifically Your initial investigations are all normal and the priapism resolves. What are your subsequent management priorities? (4 marks)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"Your patient is found to have 0/5 motor power in the lower limbs but is not insensate. What classification of spinal cord injury is this? (1 mark)"},{"id":"e","label":"Part E","prompt":"What cord syndrome is most likely to cause this pattern of injury? (1 mark)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Primary survey</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Adjuncts to primary survey (eFAST/PXR/CXR) – optional as won’t change</div><div class=\"answer-line\">management here</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Secondary survey</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Should specifically mention log roll / PR examination to check anal tone</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">CT Trauma – must mention to pass the question. CT brain/spine not</div><div class=\"answer-line\">sufficient</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">ECG</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Will accept eFAST/PXR/CXR here though again not crucial</div><div class=\"answer-line\">c. Your initial investigations are all normal and the priapism resolves. What</div><div class=\"answer-line\">are your subsequent management priorities? (4 marks)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Maintain spinal immobilisation</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Consultation with spinal injury service</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Wake/wean sedation, assess neurology</div><div class=\"answer-line\">MRI whole spine seeking SCIWORA/cord injury</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Hypertense aiming MAP &gt;85</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Will require retrieval to spinal injury centre</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">ASIA-B</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part E</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Anterior cord syndrome / anterior spinal artery syndrome</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":25,"title":"","stemText":"A 29-year-old male has had a syncopal episode while running. His ECG is displayed.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Describe and interpret the ECG (4 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"Outline the physical examination findings of aortic stenosis (3 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"Outline the difficulties around Intubation of the patient with severe aortic stenosis AND how you would mitigate them (6 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Sinus – rate 55. LVH by voltage (large amplitude QRS complexes in</div><div class=\"answer-line\">precordial leads), non-specific inferior T-wave abnormality, deep narrow Q-</div><div class=\"answer-line\">waves in the lateral leads</div><div class=\"answer-line\">High-amplitude QRS + “dagger” Q-waves = likely HCM</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Structural heart disease of several types – including aortic stenosis - is associated</div><div class=\"answer-line\">with syncope.</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Pulse – plateau pulse – small volume pulse and pulse pressure reduced</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Apex beat -hyperdynamic</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Auscultation – harsh midsystolic murmur maximal over aortic area (right</div><div class=\"answer-line\">upper sternal border) and radiating to carotids – loudest sitting up &amp; in</div><div class=\"answer-line\">expiration, splitting of S2</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Outline the difficulties around Intubation of the patient with severe</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Patients with critical AS depend on adequate left ventricular preload to</div><div class=\"answer-line\">maintain cardiac output. Both induction agents and positive pressure</div><div class=\"answer-line\">ventilation acutely drop left ventricular preload, and place patients with</div><div class=\"answer-line\">critical AS at risk for peri-intubation hemodynamic collapse. Hemodynamic</div><div class=\"answer-line\">optimization prior to induction, adequate monitoring, and selection of an</div><div class=\"answer-line\">induction agent with a favorable hemodynamic profile are the mainstays of</div><div class=\"answer-line\">safe intubation.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">As you prepare to intubate, have push-dose phenylephrine at the bedside</div><div class=\"answer-line\">(or infusing). Prior to induction, the patient should be preload optimized. If</div><div class=\"answer-line\">there is any uncertainty regarding volume status, err on the side of fluid</div><div class=\"answer-line\">administration. Even short periods of systemic hypotension can be</div><div class=\"answer-line\">devastating and are easily missed by a periodically cycling non-invasive</div><div class=\"answer-line\">blood pressure cuff. If time allows (eg, the urgent-not-emergent intubation),</div><div class=\"answer-line\">consider placing an arterial line prior to induction to decrease response time</div><div class=\"answer-line\">to systemic hypotension.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Patients with critical AS depend on the atrial kick of sinus rhythm for</div><div class=\"answer-line\">diastolic filling. If you are preparing to intubate a hemodynamically tenuous</div><div class=\"answer-line\">patient in new atrial fibrillation (or any SVT for that matter), consider</div><div class=\"answer-line\">cardioversion prior to induction.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Etomidate is the RSI induction agent of choice in patients with AS, because it</div><div class=\"answer-line\">is both hemodynamically stable and comes with a generally favorable side</div><div class=\"answer-line\">effect profile. Propofol is a profound vasodilator and can acutely drop</div><div class=\"answer-line\">preload and promote hemodynamic collapse. Ketamine promotes</div><div class=\"answer-line\">tachycardia — an unfavorable property in patients with AS.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">aortic-stenosis</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":26,"title":"","stemText":"During a busy evening shift as the consultant in charge, the NUM approaches you and asks you to speak to a patient who wishes to complain as the JMO has had 4 failed cannulation attempts and both the patient and his family are very angry and difficult to placate","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"What is your approach to receiving this live complaint? (6 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"What are the steps to the complaint management process and briefly describe each step? (8 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Ensure patient safety</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Stop before you speak – allow the person to vent and do not react defensively.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Listen for understanding – actively listen to the person making the complaint,</div><div class=\"answer-line\">empathise and acknowledge their viewpoint</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Look for solutions – express regret that they have had a poor experience.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Consider options for action to resolve this issue</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Receive / Acknowledge the complaint – acknowledge – verbal / written.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Ensure patient safety (if real time issue).</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Apologise</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Assess the complaint – severity</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Complaint management risk matrix. Documentation and notification into incident</div><div class=\"answer-line\">management system (IMMS / Riskmann).</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Consider if medicolegal / media implications – indemnity – hospital and personal</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Investigate the complaint – review records. Interview and support staff involved.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Need to document review. May be part of formal SAER / LP / CACC review or more</div><div class=\"answer-line\">formal gathering of information</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Respond / Resolve the complaint – Feedback to complainant. Verbal / written</div><div class=\"answer-line\">dependent on nature of complaint. Mediation. Formal resolution meetings</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":27,"title":"","stemText":"A 77-year-old female presents to your Emergency Department at 8pm complaining of sudden onset, persistent dizziness for the last 4 hours, associated with unsteady gait and vomiting. She has a background of hypertension and atrial fibrillation, all well controlled on oral medication including apixaban. She lives independently on her own, but her son had to call an ambulance as they could not get her to sit upright in a car.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List four (4) features on history and exam that help differentiate between a posterior circulation stroke and an Acute Vestibular Neuritis (AVN) (4 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List four (4) OTHER differential diagnoses you would consider. (4 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"You determine the patient has Acute Vestibular Neuritis after your HINTS exam. list your exam findings for each aspect of this test (3 marks)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"Outline your management of this woman (4 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">between a posterior circulation stroke and an Acute Vestibular</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Neuritis (AVN) (4 marks)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Answer:</div><div class=\"answer-line\">HISTORY</div><div class=\"answer-line\">1. Headache 30 – 40 % CVA (rarely in AVN)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2. Focal neuro CVA complaints eg. Weakness, deafness (see below)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">3. CVA risk factors (HT, DM, AF etc), but beware, young people get strokes too</div><div class=\"answer-line\">4. Viral infections/herpes for AVN</div><div class=\"answer-line\">DO NOT ACCEPT</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Continuous dizziness/vertigo, unsteady gait, nausea, vomiting – these are a</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">common feature for both syndromes</div><div class=\"answer-line\">although symptoms tend to be more debilitating with AVN</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">EXAM</div><div class=\"answer-line\">1. Focal Neurological signs of post circ CVA:</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Hemiparesis – up to 75%</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Cranial Nerve VII lesion 30-50%</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Dysarthria – 40 %</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Babinski – 30-40 %</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Ocuolomotor signs – 30-40%</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Truncal or unilateral limb ataxia – around 30%</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Sensory deficits – 20-30%</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Bilateral limb weakness – 10 -15%</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Bilateral facial weakness – 10 %</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Hearing loss – 5% (very rare in AVN, unless associated with zoster oticus)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2. Aspects of the HINTS exam</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Head impulse – lag/saccades/”positive” in AVN</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Direction of nystagmus: stroke = direction</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">changing/upbeat/downbeat/vertical, AVN = unidirectional</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Severity of nystagmus: stroke = subtle, AVN = florid</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Test of skew (rare) but positive in 10% of post circulation CVA</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">marks)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">1. Posterior circulation TIA</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2. Cerebellar haemorrhage</div><div class=\"answer-line\">3. Vertebral artery/Carotid dissection</div><div class=\"answer-line\">4. Vestibular migraine</div><div class=\"answer-line\">5. Meniere’s Disease</div><div class=\"answer-line\">6. BPPV (elderly people often do not feel they recover fully between episodes)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">7. Non – neurological cause eg. Gastroenteritis with postural dizziness,</div><div class=\"answer-line\">medications/toxins (esp cardiac meds), mental</div><div class=\"answer-line\">health/anxiety/hyperventilation</div><div class=\"answer-line\">You determine the patient has Acute Vestibular Neuritis after your HINTS exam.</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Response / justification</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Answers in bold</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>1. Head impulse positive</td><td>– corrective eye movement delay on the side of the</td></tr><tr><td>lesion</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>2. Nystagmus present</td><td>– non-fatigueable, unidirectional nystagmus – looking</td></tr><tr><td>away from the lesion</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>3. Test of Skew/Skew deviation negative</td><td>– no vertical movement/misalignment</td></tr><tr><td>of eyes with cover/uncover test</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Essentials in bold</div><div class=\"answer-line\">1. Symptom management of nausea/vomiting/vertigo (per eTG)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Listed as parenteral, as stem has patient vomiting but accept oral</div><div class=\"answer-line\">equivalents</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Specifies at least one drug and dose</div><div class=\"answer-line\">a. prochlorperazine 12.5 mg intramuscularly, immediately, followed in 6</div><div class=\"answer-line\">hours by 5 to 10 mg orally, as a single dose if needed  OR</div><div class=\"answer-line\">b. promethazine 10 to 25 mg intramuscularly or by slow intravenous</div><div class=\"answer-line\">infusion, then 10 to 25 mg orally or intramuscularly or by slow</div><div class=\"answer-line\">intravenous infusion, 8- to 12-hourly  OR</div><div class=\"answer-line\">c. ondansetron 4 to 8 mg intramuscularly or by slow intravenous</div><div class=\"answer-line\">injection, 8- to 12-hourly</div><div class=\"answer-line\">d. Trial of fluids/oral intake +/- IVF if required</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2. Treatment of AVN (per eTG)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">a. Usually will self-resolve (hours to days)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">b. If severe, consider steroids as may hasten recovery, evidence not</div><div class=\"answer-line\">clear</div><div class=\"answer-line\">i.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">prednis(ol)one 1 mg/kg (up to 75 mg) orally, daily in the</div><div class=\"answer-line\">morning for 5 days, then taper dose over 15 days and stop.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">c. Anti-virals shown no benefit (unless actually caused by acute herpes</div><div class=\"answer-line\">oticus)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">3. Disposition planning (per common sense)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">a. Physio/ASET/Clinician – check mobility</div><div class=\"answer-line\">b. EMU/SSU/Neuro admission if not safe for discharge</div><div class=\"answer-line\">c. Advice to patient/carer/relative – eg. Factsheets on ECI, about</div><div class=\"answer-line\">medication eg. Steroid wean, take in morning etc, dangers of vertigo –</div><div class=\"answer-line\">no driving, safety etc</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false}];
